course: dictionary
lesson: 12549
title: Words that stop things happening
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Deterrence is a noun. It means stopping people from doing something by making them afraid of what would happen if they did it. Look for clues in the sentence. «The town put up CCTV cameras, and the thieves stayed away.» The cameras make thieves afraid of being caught, so they stop. That is deterrence.
explain: Punishments and fines are a kind of deterrence. «Breaking the speed limit can bring a big fine.» The fine is there so drivers slow down before they break the rule. Ask yourself: is there a bad thing that might happen? If yes, the word may be deterrence.
explain: In history and politics, the word has a special use. «Nuclear deterrence» means a country keeps powerful weapons, hoping it will never have to use them. The idea is that an attacker who knows they would be hit back will not attack. To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence: "the threat that stops people" should still make sense.
fact: Deterrence means stopping people from doing something by making them afraid of what would happen.
fact: A «deterrent» is the thing that does the stopping, such as a sign warning that CCTV is in use.
fact: «Nuclear deterrence» means countries keep weapons so that an attacker knows it would be hit back.
fact: Fines and punishments are often used as deterrence, to stop people breaking rules.
exercise: «The town put up cameras as a deterrence against thieves.» What does deterrence mean here? | *Stopping thieves by making them afraid of being caught | Helping thieves find their way around town | Taking photographs of the town for a holiday album | A loud alarm that wakes everyone up
exercise: Why are there fines for breaking school rules? | To give the school more money for sweets | *To stop pupils breaking rules by making them afraid of the punishment | To make pupils have a party | To make pupils happy and carefree
exercise: Which of these is a deterrent to thieves? | A sunny day at the park | A cheerful song | *A sign saying CCTV is in use | A bunch of flowers
test: «Nuclear deterrence» means countries keep weapons so that an attacker will | Forget that the weapons exist | Give away their weapons to friends | Start attacking as soon as possible | *Stop attacking, because they know they would be hit back
test: What is the best way to check the meaning of a new word? | Read it aloud only once | Ignore the sentence and guess | *Swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense | Count how many letters it has
exam: Which sentence uses deterrence correctly? | *«The big fine was a deterrence to people who might speed.» | «She drank a deterrence of orange juice.» | «The deterrence sang happily in the garden.» | «The deterrence was a sunny afternoon at the beach.»
